
wildcards are special characters used by the shell to match filenames and paths. They are also called globbing patterns.



Shell Wildcards
===============

| Character | Meaning                            | Example             | Matches                               |
| --------- | ---------------------------------- | ------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| `*`       | Zero or more characters            | `ls *.txt`          | `a.txt`, `file.txt`, `backup.txt`     |
| `?`       | Exactly one character              | `ls file?.txt`      | `file1.txt`, `fileA.txt`              |
| `[abc]`   | One character from the set         | `ls file[abc].txt`  | `filea.txt`, `fileb.txt`, `filec.txt` |
| `[a-z]`   | Any lowercase letter               | `ls file[a-z].txt`  | `filea.txt` – `filez.txt`             |
| `[A-Z]`   | Any uppercase letter               | `ls file[A-Z].txt`  | `fileA.txt` – `fileZ.txt`             |
| `[0-9]`   | Any digit                          | `ls file[0-9].txt`  | `file0.txt` – `file9.txt`             |
| `[!abc]`  | Any character except `a`, `b`, `c` | `ls file[!abc].txt` | `filed.txt`, `file1.txt`              |
| `\`       | Escape character                   | `ls my\ file.txt`   | `my file.txt`                         |




Regular Expressions (Regex)
===========================


| Regex   | Meaning                     | Example                          | What it matches                    |
| ------- | --------------------------- | -------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| `^`     | Beginning of line           | `grep '^root' file`              | Lines starting with `root`         |
| `$`     | End of line                 | `grep 'bash$' file`              | Lines ending with `bash`           |
| `.`     | Any single character        | `grep 'c.t' file`                | `cat`, `cot`, `cut`                |
| `+`     | One or more occurrences     | `grep -E 'ab+' file`             | `ab`, `abb`, `abbb`                |
| `\|`    | OR                          | `grep -E 'error\|failed' file`   | `error` or `failed`                |
| `()`    | Grouping                    | `grep -E '(error\|failed)' file` | `error` or `failed`                |
| `[]`    | Character set               | `grep '[abc]' file`              | `a`, `b`, or `c`                   |
| `[^]`   | Character NOT in set        | `grep '[^abc]' file`             | Any character except `a`, `b`, `c` |
| `*`     | Zero or more occurrences    | `grep -E 'ab*' file`             | `a`, `ab`, `abb`, `abbb`           |
| `?`     | Zero or one occurrence      | `grep -E 'ab?' file`             | `a`, `ab`                          |
| `{n}`   | Exactly n occurrences       | `grep -E 'a{3}' file`            | `aaa`                              |
| `{n,m}` | Between n and m occurrences | `grep -E 'a{2,4}' file`          | `aa`, `aaa`, `aaaa`                |





